/**
 * T3 city page — Stanton, Martin County. About 90 road miles south-east of
 * Seminole, on Interstate 20 between Midland and Big Spring.
 *
 * ############################################################################
 * BY-REQUEST PAGE. SHIPPED Aug 2026. Read this before editing a word of it.
 *
 * This page was written as a normal city page, then held at noindex because
 * D-07 said the owner WANTED Stanton but did not serve it. In August 2026 he
 * answered the question directly: "Have not currently serviced yet but would
 * if a call came through."
 *
 * That is not coverage, and it is not nothing. So the page was rewritten to
 * claim exactly what is true — he does not run a Martin County route, and he
 * will drive it if someone calls — and shipped on that basis.
 *
 * THE RULES THIS PAGE LIVES BY, all of which are easy to erode by accident:
 *  - It must never imply routine service, a existing customer base, a Stanton
 *    call history, or a schedule that already passes through the town.
 *  - Pest-pressure copy describes what MARTIN COUNTY PROPERTY deals with,
 *    sourced from entomology and the quarantine, NOT what he has been called
 *    about here. He has not been called about anything here.
 *  - The by-request framing appears above the fold, not buried in a closing
 *    caveat. A disclaimer at the bottom of a page that reads as a coverage
 *    claim at the top is a coverage claim.
 *  - If he starts running Stanton properly, this page gets rewritten UP into a
 *    normal city page. Until then it does not drift upward one sentence at a
 *    time.
 * ############################################################################
 *
 * VERIFIED facts used here:
 *  - Ordinance: Stanton Code ch. 7 art. 7.03, "Stagnant Water, Filth and
 *    Tall Weeds and Grass" (eCode360, current through 8 July 2024),
 *    §§ 7.03.001–7.03.008. Enforcement by the city administrator-secretary
 *    at 7.03.001. 7.03.002 deems weeds or grass at TWELVE INCHES OR MORE
 *    unsanitary, and covers stagnant water, carrion, filth and other impure
 *    or unwholesome matter in the same breath. SEVEN days' notice at
 *    7.03.004, which also carries the one-year repeat-violation provision.
 *    City correction and charge at 7.03.005. Statement of expenses filed
 *    with the COUNTY CLERK by the mayor at 7.03.006. PRIVILEGED LIEN
 *    "second only to tax liens" at TEN PERCENT per annum from filing, at
 *    7.03.007. Misdemeanour penalty at 7.03.008.
 *  - 7.03.003 — the sight-line and clearance standard, unique on this
 *    route: vegetation may not obstruct pedestrian or motorist safety or
 *    traffic visibility within 100 FEET; bushes in parkways may not exceed
 *    TWO AND A HALF FEET above gutter elevation; trees must keep SEVEN feet
 *    of clearance above ground and EIGHT feet above sidewalks.
 *  - Population 2,657 (2020 census); ACS 5-year 2,638. Housing units 994;
 *    households 939; homeownership 78.4% — the HIGHEST on this route.
 *    Below the 5,000 threshold, so no Census QuickFacts page exists.
 *  - Geography: elevation 2,664 ft. Total area 1.99 sq mi. On I-20 between
 *    Midland and Big Spring; Martin County is ~25 miles north-east of
 *    Midland on the southern High Plains, above the escarpment Big Spring
 *    sits below. Soils primarily RED SANDY LOAM, with hackberry, mesquite
 *    and willow. County elevation 2,550–3,000 ft. Rainfall 15.72 in/yr;
 *    January lows average 30°F, July highs 95°F.
 *  - Economy: agriculture and oil and gas. Crops cotton, small grains and
 *    corn; county agriculture ~$36M annually, ~90% from those three.
 *    Stanton oilfield production began 1951; Martin County oil peaked at
 *    11.8 million barrels in 1974. Martin County Hospital District.
 *  - History: established 1881 as Grelton by German Catholic settlers led
 *    by John Jacob Konz and Father Christian D. Peters; renamed Marienfeld
 *    1885; renamed Stanton 1890 for Edwin McMasters Stanton. First Catholic
 *    church in West Texas built within months of arrival. Sisters of Mercy
 *    founded the Convent and Academy of Our Lady of Mercy in 1894, for
 *    years the only Catholic academy between Fort Worth and El Paso; the
 *    monastery relocated to Louisiana in 1897. John Scharbauer established
 *    a cattle ranch nearby in 1887. A $40,000 courthouse dates to 1910.
 *    Incorporated mid-1920s (TSHA says 1925, the chamber says 1926 — the
 *    page says "the mid-1920s"). Old Sorehead Trade Days runs twice a year,
 *    the second weekend in April and October, ~615 booths, ~30,000
 *    visitors. Town tagline: "3,000 Friendly People and a few Old
 *    Soreheads."
 *  - Martin County IS on the red imported fire ant quarantine adopted by
 *    reference at 4 TAC §19.101 (APHIS regulated-areas list). An earlier draft
 *    of this page said the opposite. CORRECTED Aug 2026.
 *  - NO pest control company is listed at a Stanton street address. Yellow
 *    Pages returns 15 Midland firms, 6 Odessa firms, Southwestern A-1 from
 *    Big Spring, plus Lubbock, Abilene, Amarillo and Pecos. Stanton is
 *    served entirely from out of town — a defensible, verifiable claim.
 *
 * NOT FOUND — MUST NOT BE STATED:
 *  - Median year structure built; published vacancy rate.
 *  - Named employers.
 *  - Named neighbourhoods or subdivisions (explicitly none documented).
 *  - Flooding or drainage history.
 *  - Exact I-20 mileage from Stanton to Midland or Big Spring.
 *  - Exact road mileage from Seminole. 64.5 mi is the STRAIGHT-LINE figure and
 *    must not be published as a drive; the road route runs out through Andrews
 *    and back east, so the page says "about ninety miles by road" instead.
 *
 * TODO(fact): D-09 named no Stanton neighbourhoods. Do not invent any.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AnswerBox from '../../components/AnswerBox.astro';
import Trust from '../../components/Trust.astro';
import ServiceMap from '../../components/ServiceMap.astro';
import Crumbs from '../../components/Crumbs.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import Cta from '../../components/Cta.astro';
import { SERVICES } from '../../data/services';

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Areas we serve', path: '/locations/' },
  { name: 'Stanton', path: '/locations/stanton/' },
];

const quickAnswer =
  'Trusted Termite and Pest Solutions is not based in Stanton and does not run a regular Martin County route. Stanton work happens by request: you call, and owner Valentin Moriel drives the ninety miles from Seminole and does the job himself under Texas licence TPCL 0976265.';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Do you actually service Stanton?',
    a: 'By request, yes — and we are going to be precise about what that means, because the difference matters when you are the one waiting. We do not have a Stanton route. We are not in Martin County on a schedule, we have no existing customers there, and nobody drives past your house on a Tuesday. What we have is a licence, a truck and ninety miles of road. If you call and the work can be planned — a termite treatment, a recurring service you want started, a job between tenants, several properties grouped into one trip — then it gets booked as a planned day and it gets done properly. If you need somebody this afternoon, call a Midland company; they are twenty-five miles away and we are not.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How tall can grass and weeds get in Stanton?',
    a: 'Twelve inches. Section 7.03.002 deems weeds or grass growing to a height of twelve inches or more to be unsanitary, and the same section covers stagnant water and the accumulation of carrion, filth or other impure or unwholesome matter. The article is headed "Stagnant Water, Filth and Tall Weeds and Grass," which tells you the city treats all three as one problem.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long do I get after a notice in Stanton?',
    a: 'Seven days, under section 7.03.004. The same section allows the notice to state that repeated violations within one year may be corrected by the city at your expense without additional notice — so one letter can cover twelve months.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens if the city has to do the work?',
    a: 'Under 7.03.005 the city may correct the condition and charge you the expenses. Under 7.03.006 the mayor files a statement of those expenses with the county clerk. Under 7.03.007 the city then holds a privileged lien on the property, second only to tax liens, bearing ten percent per annum from the date of filing. It is a misdemeanour as well, under 7.03.008.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are there rules about trees and bushes in Stanton?',
    a: 'Yes, and they are more specific than anywhere else on our route. Section 7.03.003 prohibits vegetation that obstructs pedestrian or motorist safety or traffic visibility within one hundred feet. Bushes in parkways may not exceed two and a half feet above gutter elevation. Trees must maintain seven feet of clearance above the ground and eight feet above sidewalks. If you have a shrub at a corner or a low branch over a walk, those are the actual numbers.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a pest control company based in Stanton?',
    a: 'No. Directory searches return no business with a Stanton street address — every listing is a company serving Stanton from Midland, Odessa, Big Spring, Lubbock, Abilene, Amarillo or Pecos. Stanton is served entirely from out of town, which means the honest question is not who is local but who is willing to actually come back when you need them again.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I have fire ants in Stanton?',
    a: 'Possibly — and Stanton is one of the few places on our route where the answer is not simply no. Martin County is on the red imported fire ant quarantine, as are Midland, Ector and Howard around it. We read that off the APHIS regulated-areas list ourselves in August 2026 rather than taking anybody\'s summary for it, after an earlier version of this page got it the wrong way round and a later one hedged. The quarantine is federal: 4 TAC §19.101 adopts the current APHIS regulated-areas list by reference rather than naming counties itself, so the live list is the one that governs. Practically it means two things. Fire ants are a genuine possibility here rather than a misidentification, and if you are moving soil, sod, nursery stock, baled hay stored on the ground or used soil-moving equipment out of Martin County into a non-quarantined area, the movement rules apply to you.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How far is Stanton from you?',
    a: 'About ninety miles by road to the south-east. The straight line is shorter, but the route runs out through Andrews and back east, so ninety is the honest number rather than the flattering one. At that distance Stanton is not somewhere we pass through — a job here is a day arranged around it, which is why notice matters more than it would in Seminole or Andrews.',
  },
  {
    q: '¿Ofrecen servicio en español en Stanton?',
    a: 'Sí. Valentin habla español con fluidez y hace el trabajo él mismo. Para que quede claro: no tenemos ruta fija en Stanton ni clientes allí todavía. El trabajo en Stanton se hace por solicitud — usted llama y se programa un día completo para el viaje de noventa millas. Por eso unos días de aviso cambian mucho las cosas. Llame o mande un texto al (432) 278-7294.',
